[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 518,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 497,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73270,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 497,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Rosai&ndash;Dorfman disease of the CNS or the meninges, with or without systemic lesions, pathologically corresponds to its counterparts occurring elsewhere. It is a clonal histiocytic proliferation characterized by large S100-positive histiocytes with variable emperipolesis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:17:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 519,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 498,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73271,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 498,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9749/3 Rosai&ndash;Dorfman disease</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:17:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 520,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 499,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73272,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 499,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EK92 Histiocytoses of uncertain malignant potential</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:17:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 521,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 500,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73273,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 500,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> sinus histiocytosis with massive lymphadenopathy.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:17:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 522,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 501,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73274,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 501,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:17:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 523,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 502,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73275,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 502,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Rosai&ndash;Dorfman disease of the CNS forms solitary or multiple dural masses, especially in the cerebral convexity, cranial base, and cavernous sinuses, as well as parasagittal, suprasellar and petroclival regions {25183389}. Parenchymal or intrasellar lesions may also occur.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:17:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 524,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 503,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73276,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 503,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients may exhibit signs of increased intracranial pressure or focal neurological deficits. Patients with sellar lesions present with signs of hypopituitarism and diabetes insipidus. The classic systemic signs&nbsp;&ndash; cervical lymphadenopathy, fever, and weight loss&nbsp;&ndash; are absent in 70% of patients, and 52% have no associated systemic disease {16099162}. On MRI, Rosai&ndash;Dorfman disease resembles meningioma. Lesions are isointense or hypointense on T1-weighted images, and they show homogeneous contrast enhancement. Hypointensity on T2-weighted images may help in the differential diagnosis between Rosai&ndash;Dorfman disease and classic meningioma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:17:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 525,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 504,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73277,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 504,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The M:F&nbsp;ratio is estimated at 2:1, and the mean age of patients with CNS Rosai&ndash;Dorfman disease is approximately 40&nbsp;years {24797172}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:17:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 526,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 505,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73278,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 505,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:17:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 527,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 506,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73279,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 506,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Two recent studies found <em>BRAF</em> p.V600E (in 12.5% of cases) and mutations in <em>KRAS</em> or <em>NRAS</em> (in 25% and 12.5% of cases, respectively) {31768065; 26566875}. Single cases with <em>ARAF</em>, <em>MAP2K1</em>, and <em>CSF1R</em> mutations have been reported {31768065}. However, the numbers of Rosai&ndash;Dorfman cases were limited in these high-throughput sequencing analyses of histiocytic disorders, and Rosai&ndash;Dorfman samples were among those types in which no alteration could be detected in a large proportion.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:17:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 534,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 513,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73280,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 513,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Rosai&ndash;Dorfman disease of the CNS is typically a firm, vaguely lobulated, yellow to greyish-white dural mass.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:17:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 528,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 507,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73281,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 507,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Rosai&ndash;Dorfman disease occurs as a multinodular mass composed of a mixed inflammatory infiltrate including large pale histiocytes, numerous lymphocytes and plasma cells, and variable fibrosis. Emperipolesis with histiocytic engulfment of intact lymphocytes, plasma cells, neutrophils, and occasionally eosinophils is typical, but it may be inconspicuous or absent. Notably, emperipolesis is not pathognomonic of Rosai&ndash;Dorfman disease, occasionally being encountered in other neoplastic or non-neoplastic disorders.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunophenotype</h5>\r\n<p>The neoplastic histiocytes are positive for CD11c, CD68, CD163, fascin, and S100; variably positive for lysozyme; and negative for CD1a and CD207 (langerin). Expression of cyclin&nbsp;D1 (possibly reflecting MAPK activation) can be diagnostically useful {31172509}, particularly because most cases are negative for BRAF p.V600E.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:17:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 529,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 508,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73282,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 508,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not applicable</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:17:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 530,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 509,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73283,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 509,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Testing for potentially mutant genes (<em>BRAF</em>, <em>ARAF</em>, <em>KRAS</em> and <em>NRAS</em>, and the rare<em> MAP2K1</em> and <em>CSF1R</em>) should be performed with sensitive assays, reflecting the frequently low proportion of neoplastic cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:17:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 533,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
